This is actually equivalent to using the last mipping record. This may modify the previous mapping. 
Because `add_joy_mapping()` can choose not to update the existing mapping.

```
offset    -1            -2          -3              -4
----------r0----ai--aj--r1---am------r2-----an------r3--------------
```
```
--------------ai--aj-----am-----------an--------------------
```

I suggest using an array `removed_idx` to record the removed indexes (`r0`, `r1`, .. `r3`), and then compare the removed indexes here.

```cpp
for (KeyValue<int, Joypad> &E : joy_names) {
	Joypad &joy = E.value;
	if (joy.mapping < min_removed_idx) {
		continue; // Not affected.
	}

	if (joy.mapping > max_removed_idx) {
		_set_joypad_mapping(joy, joy.mapping - removed_idx_size);
		continue; // Simple offset.
	}

	// removed_idx is in reverse order because the first loop is in reverse order.
	for (int i = 0; i < removed_idx.size(); i++) {
		if (removed_idx[i] == joy.mapping) {
			_set_joypad_mapping(joy, fallback_mapping); // fallback_mapping need update first.
			break; // Removed, including the case where it is used as fallback_mapping.
		}
		if (removed_idx[i] < joy.mapping) {
			_set_joypad_mapping(joy, joy.mapping - (removed_idx_size - i));
			break; // Affected indexes.
		}
	}
}
```

